  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Monday)\nIssued at 120 PM MDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nIt's another wait and watch the sky day as more strong to severe\nstorms are looking likely. Latest hi res model guidance has\nthunderstorm initiation happening by 2 to 4PM near the northern \nBlack Hills and in NW SD. As of this writing (12:30PM), clouds are\nstarting to bubble up over the central and northern Black Hills, \nindications that the CAP is slowly being eroded away. Using the \nHRRR Convective Hazard Forecast, the time frame from 4PM to 8PM \nhas the highest chances for severe weather in western SD, just \neast of the Black Hills. From there, probabilities diminish and \ntrack southeast to near zero by 10PM in southern SD.\n\nWhen these storms get going, they are expected to track southeast\nthrough the western SD plains. Main concerns now are large hail,\nthen strong winds. Isolated tornadoes can't be ruled out at this\ntime.\n\nWednesday is the beginning of a big pattern change over the area.\nUpper level high pressure will slide southeast, allowing for \nmoisture to flow into the area due to southwest flow. For now, \nexpecting clouds to build and last through Friday. So a cloudy few\ndays are in store. Thunderstorms are also likely tomorrow\nafternoon and evening and we can't rule out severe weather. At \nthe surface, moist, easterly flow north of a developing surface \nlow in eastern Colorado/western Kansas will allow for moderate to \nstrong destabilization across parts of Nebraska/southern South \nDakota. Steep mid-level lapse rates and moderate to strong \neffective shear will promote supercell development from near the \nBlack Hills and south into Nebraska. Large hail and isolated \nsevere wind gusts would be possible initially.\n\nContinued rain and storm chances last for the rest of the week\ntoo.",
